        <dc:title>Cloud-Native Hybrid Deployment Technology Under Narrowband Communication Conditions</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Edge computing extends cloud capabilities to the network periphery to reduce latency and bandwidth usage, but implementing cloud-native technologies in these environments poses unique challenges. This paper surveys cloud-native hybrid deployment strategies under narrowband communication conditions, as encountered in IoT and remote edge scenarios. We review the state of the art in general-purpose edge computing, focusing on containerization and orchestration frameworks that bridge cloud and edge deployments despite constrained network bandwidth. Key platforms and tools are examined in depth, including Kubernetes for container orchestration, lightweight Kubernetes variants like K3s and edge-specific frameworks like KubeEdge, package management with Helm, GitOps continuous delivery via Argo CD, event-driven scaling with KEDA, and the integration of security through DevSecOps practices. We describe architectural approaches for hybrid cloud-edge orchestration, evaluate each solution's design, and discuss how they accommodate intermittent connectivity and low data rates. Through careful analysis of existing literature and industry practices, we highlight how cloud-native paradigms can be adapted to bandwidth-constrained edge environments, identify open challenges in managing deployments over narrowband links, and outline future research directions in optimizing cloud-edge synergy under network limitations.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2025-07</dc:date>
